      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Coronary heart disease (CHD) in familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) may be modified by genetic and/or environmental factors. We described the effect of the cholesteryl ester transfer protein (CETP) gene on CHD in heterozygous FH caused by low density lipoprotein receptor (LDL-R) gene mutation. In 288 unrelated Japanese subjects with heterozygous FH, the allele frequency of an intron 14 G(+1)-to-A mutation (Int14 A) and a missense mutation in exon 15 (Asp442 to Gly, D442G) was 0.3 and 3.0%, respectively. HDL-C levels (1.55 +/- 0.08 mmol/l) in FH patients with heterozygous CETP deficiency were higher than those (1.19 +/- 0.08 mmol/l) in FH without CETP deficiency (P < 0.03), while LDL-C levels in FH with CETP deficiency were moderately reduced. However, two FH patients with CETP deficiency suffered myocardial infarction, and six patients had effort angina pectoris and/or coronary atherosclerosis. No difference in the score of coronary stenosis index (CSI) was found in FH with/without CETP deficiency, although CSI was inversely correlated with HDL-C levels (P < 0.05). Thus, the effect of increased HDL-C levels caused by partial deficiency of CETP is insufficient to prevent CHD in FH."},
